prompt.facts:
        Use ONLY these facts. If a fact is "(not captured)", do not guess it.
        - Failing check (for your understanding only, never copy its wording): {rule_summary}
        - Technical detail from the scanner (for your understanding only): {technical_description}
        - WCAG criterion being tested: {sc_code} {sc_name} (Level {level}, {principle})
        - Page URL: {page_url}
        - Page title: {page_title}
        - What the screen reader says for this element today: {current_announcement}
        - Where the element sits on the page (landmark, heading above it, position, Tab presses): 
{element_context}
        - Screen reader quick key for this kind of element: {screen_reader_quick_key}
        - Times the same problem repeats on this page: {repeat_count}
        - Element HTML:
{element_html}

field_guidelines.expected_result:
3 to 5 sentences. Always include all three parts. Also ALWAYS include a written-out example of what the screen reader should announce, in quotes
(for example: "Graphic, person using a trading application"). Build the example only from the facts; it must match the real content and purpose of the element.
If you cannot know the exact wording, say "for example" and keep it sensible:
(a) the correct state, in plain words (for a missing title: "The page should have a clear title that matches what the page is about");
(b) what a screen reader user would hear or be able to do when it is correct;
(c) the benefit once fixed (saves time, avoids guessing, feels confident, can use the page on their own).

field_guidelines.actual_result:
3 to 5 sentences. Start with "When the screen reader user reaches this element, ..." and say exactly what is announced today in quotes (for example: only "Graphic").
Say clearly what information is NOT given. Always include all three parts:
(a) what is really on this element today (quote the real text or say what is missing);
(b) what the screen reader user hears or cannot do because of it;
(c) how that feels for them, or how to test if it is intentional (Version A vs Version B).

examples.example:
{{
    "description": "This page has no title. The title is the name a screen reader reads out first, so people know which page they are on.",
    "expected_result": "The page should have a clear title that matches what the page is about, for example 'Contact Us - Example Bank'. When the page opens, a screen reader user hears this title straight away and knows they are in the right place. This saves time and avoids guessing, especially when many browser tabs are open.",
    "actual_result": "The page has no title. A screen reader user hears only the web address or the word 'untitled' when the page opens. They cannot tell what the page is about or which tab they are on, so they feel lost and have to explore the page to find out.",
    "steps_to_reproduce": [
        "Open https://www.example.com/contact in your browser.",
        "Start NVDA or JAWS.",
        "Press Insert+T in JAWS or NVDA+T in NVDA to hear the page title.",
        "Listen to what is announced.",
        "Notice that you hear only the web address or 'untitled' instead of a name that describes the page."
    ]
}}

{{
    "description": "The round icon button next to the search box has no label. A person who cannot see the icon has no way to know what the button does.",
    "expected_result": "The button should have a short label such as 'Open filters'. A screen reader user would hear 'Open filters, button' and know what happens if they press it. They can then use the filters on their own without asking for help.",
    "actual_result": "The button has no label at all. A screen reader user hears only 'button', with no hint about its purpose. They have to guess or press it to find out, which feels unsafe and slows them down.",
    "steps_to_reproduce": [
        "Open https://www.example.com/search in your browser.",
        "Start NVDA or JAWS.",
        "Press D in NVDA or R in JAWS until you hear 'search'.",
        "Press F to move to the search box, then press Tab once to move to the next control.",
        "Listen to what is announced.",
        "Notice that you hear only 'button' and nothing that explains what it does."
    ]
}}

prompt.self_check:
Before answering, confirm: no banned word is used; the real element text or label is quoted when it exists;
expected_result has the benefit; actual_result has the feeling; steps start with opening the page and end with what is noticed;
nothing is invented.

prompt.output_format:
Return ONLY one valid raw JSON object. No markdown fences, no text before or after it.
{{
    "description": "",
    "expected_result": "",
    "actual_result": "",
    "steps_to_reproduce": ["", ""]
}}
